DESCRIPTION:A conversation with the sculptor Dimpy Menon reflecting on her artistic journey and the evolution of her practice. In dialogue with Reema Moudgil\, she will speak about the ideas\, influences and experiences that have informed her work\, her sustained engagement with the human figure\, sculpture and material. The conversation offers insights into the questions that have shaped her practice\, and the ways it has developed over the course of her career.\n\nThis event is in collaboration with Ananya Drishya
DTEND:20260918T130000Z
DTSTART:20260918T120000Z
SUMMARY:Sculpting the Human Form: Art\, Material and Practice
UID:1398@map-india.org
URL:https://map-india.org/map-events/sculpting-the-human-form-art-material-and-practice/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://map-india.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/D70-sofa-by-Tecno-design-Osvaldo-Borsani.jpg
CATEGORIES:Talks,MAP,CBD
DTSTAMP:20260902T093913Z
DESCRIPTION:Studio Ballo+Ballo\, 1954\, Divano D70\, Osvaldo Borsani for Tecno\nJoin us for a talk with historian and curator Libby Sellers exploring how twentieth-century design became iconic not simply through the objects themselves\, but through the visual ecosystems that surrounded them: photography\, magazines\, catalogues\, advertising\, exhibitions\, museums and collecting. \n\nBeginning with John Berger’s Ways of Seeing and the idea that every image embodies a particular way of seeing\, the talk considers how images have shaped our understanding of what design looks like\, what it means\, and which objects and designers become part of the cultural canon.\n\nFrom post-war Italian design through Californian architecture\, it asks how photography transforms both the built environment and our understanding of it. The talk then brings this framework into a South Asian context through the architect Minnette de Silva and the journal MARG\, exploring the role of publishing in making a regional modernism visible and intelligible\, while opening questions around gender\, authorship and the construction of the design canon. Drawing on research into twentieth-century furniture\, women designers and collectible design\, the talk asks who gets photographed\, published\, collected and remembered\, and who remains outside the frame. \n\nUltimately\, the talk asks: when we think we recognise an iconic object\, are we recognising the object itself\, or the history of images that has taught us how to see it?
DTEND:20260925T123000Z
DTSTART:20260925T113000Z
SUMMARY:How Objects Become Icons: Photography\, Publishing and the Making of Design History
UID:1395@map-india.org
URL:https://map-india.org/map-events/how-objects-become-icons-photography-publishing-and-the-making-of-design-history/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://map-india.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/MAP-Website.jpg
CATEGORIES:Workshops,MAP,CBD
DTSTAMP:20260904T043453Z
DESCRIPTION:What can a film poster reveal when we take the time to really look at it?\n\nFacilitated by Rajesh Devraj and Ashish Rajadhyaksha\, this intimate\, off-the-cuff session invites participants to gather around a table and closely examine a selection of film posters\, unpacking the many layers contained within each image. Through conversation and collective looking\, we’ll explore the artistic styles\, printing techniques\, regional variations\, typography\, visual conventions and business strategies that shaped these objects—and the ways in which they communicated with audiences.
DTEND:20260911T103000Z
DTSTART:20260911T093000Z
SUMMARY:Reading the Film Poster
UID:1397@map-india.org
URL:https://map-india.org/map-events/reading-the-film-poster/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://map-india.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Assitive-Tech-2.jpg
CATEGORIES:Mixer,MAP,CBD
DTSTAMP:20260904T101356Z
DESCRIPTION:What happens when we think about access in museums not simply as a question of entry\, but as a question of how we experience\, participate\, navigate and belong?\n\nMaking Art\, Making Access brings together artists\, disabled practitioners\, museum professionals\, accessibility practitioners\, researchers\, designers and technologists for an afternoon of conversation\, exchange and exploration.\n\nTogether\, we will ask:\n\nHow do bodies\, identities and lived experiences shape the ways we participate in cultural spaces?\n\nHow do design\, technology\, services\, policy and research influence who can access and navigate museums?\n\nWhat can museums do differently to create more meaningful and inclusive experiences?\n\nThe afternoon brings together artistic practice\, disability scholarship\, museum research and practical approaches to accessibility. Participants will also have an opportunity to engage with research emerging from MAP’s work on museum accessibility and technology\, offering insights into audience needs\, existing gaps and possibilities for action.\n\nMore than a series of talks\, the mixer is an opportunity to meet like-minded people\, exchange ideas and build connections across art\, culture\, accessibility\, technology and design.\n\nMaking Art\, Making Access is part of MAP’s ongoing work to learn\, experiment and rethink what accessibility can look like in museums - and an invitation to imagine those possibilities together.\n\n&nbsp\;\n\nPlease find the programme below:\n\n2:00 – 2:10 PM: Welcome &amp\; Setting the Context\nMAP \n\nAn introduction to the afternoon and the central question: How can artists and museums adopt technology to create more accessible ways of experiencing art?\n\n2:10 – 2:45 PM: Artist-led Keynote by Akhila Vimal C.\n\nA practice-led exploration of technology\, adaptation and artistic practice\, and how these can challenge conventional ideas of how art is created\, experienced and accessed.\n\n2:45 – 3:00 PM: Pause &amp\; Reset\n\nA short\, participatory pause to refresh\, reset and reconnect before the next session.\n\n3:00 – 3:45 PM: Museum Accessibility Practices : A map for MAP by Gaurav Raheja\n\nExploring the intersection of museums\, information and communication design\, technology\, services\, and legal and policy frameworks. \n\nDrawing on examples from museums around the world\, the session will examine how these different systems come together to shape who can access\, navigate and participate in cultural spaces—and what institutions can learn from emerging approaches to inclusive design and accessibility.\n\n3:45 – 4:00 PM: Coffee / Tea Break\n\nA short break and an opportunity for informal conversations and connections.\n\n4:00 – 4:45 PM: From Possibility to Practice by Mudito\n\nDrawing on Mudito’s accessibility and technology research with MAP\, this session explores audience needs\, existing gaps and opportunities\, and how museums can translate accessibility and technology into practical institutional action.\n\n4:45 – 5:00 PM: Pause &amp\; Reset\n\nA short\, participatory pause to refresh and reset before the final session.\n\n5:00 – 5:45 PM: One Platform\, Many Museums by Bloomberg Connects\n\nAn exploration of how shared digital infrastructure can help museums adopt technology\, expand access to collections and exhibitions\, and create more accessible pathways to interpretation and engagement.\n\n5:45 – 6:15 PM: Closing: What Do We Take Forward? \n\nA closing reflection bringing together the afternoon’s conversations and identifying key learnings\, potential collaborations and directions for MAP’s future work on accessibility and technology.\n\nWho is this for?\n\nThe programme is intended for artists\, museum and cultural professionals\, accessibility practitioners\, disabled people and disability organisations\, technology and digital experience professionals\, researchers\, designers\, educators and students interested in rethinking access to art and culture.\n\nJoin us for an afternoon of conversations\, demonstrations\, shared learning and new possibilities for making art and museums more accessible.
DTEND:20260912T130000Z
DTSTART:20260912T083000Z
SUMMARY:Making Art\, Making Access
UID:1394@map-india.org
URL:https://map-india.org/map-events/making-art-making-access/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://map-india.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/POP.23535.3.jpg
CATEGORIES:Book Launch,MAP,CBD
DTSTAMP:20260902T111419Z
DESCRIPTION:What can a lobby card tell us about the ways we have imagined\, remembered and encountered Indian cinema?\n\nMarking the launch of the publication ‘The Momentary and the Monumental’\, this panel brings together contributors Ashish Rajadhyaksha\, Debashree Mukherjee and Iyesha Geeth Abbas to reflect on the histories\, aesthetics and material lives of popular cinema publicity. Moving beyond the films themselves\, the conversation will consider lobby cards and related ephemera as carefully constructed visual objects—shaped by photographers\, artists\, designers\, printers and the conditions of their circulation.\n\nDrawing on their contributions to the publication\, the speakers will explore what these often-overlooked images reveal about the visual language of Indian cinema\, the cultures of print and publicity that surrounded it\, and the ways in which cinematic memory is produced beyond the screen. The discussion will also open up questions around the archives and histories that remain to be uncovered\, and what it means to look closely at the material traces of popular cinema.\n\n&nbsp\;
DTEND:20260911T140000Z
DTSTART:20260911T130000Z
SUMMARY:The Momentary and the Monumental - Publication Launch
UID:1396@map-india.org
URL:https://map-india.org/map-events/the-momentary-and-the-monumental-publication-launch/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://map-india.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/04-scaled.jpg
CATEGORIES:Youth Engagement,MAP,CBD
DTSTAMP:20260903T044544Z
DESCRIPTION:Drawing from MAP’s textile collection\, this workshop explores how textiles can carry memories through colour\, material and repair. Using a curated selection of objects\, including kantha\, patchwork\, appliqué\, quilting\, embroidered purses\, shawls\, saris and shade cards\, participants will closely observe the traces of making\, mending\, wear and care embedded within each object.\n\nBeginning with observation\, participants will reflect on the personal associations these textiles evoke\, whether it is a grandmother’s quilt\, a mother’s sari\, a well-worn shawl or a favourite piece of clothing. These memories become a starting point for developing a visual vocabulary of colours\, marks and rhythms that responds to the material qualities of the objects rather than reproducing them.\n\nRegistration for the event will open soon!\n\nThis programme is exclusively for 18–25 year olds.\n\nThis session is conducted as part of our engagements for the MAP Youth Collective. The MAP Youth Collective is a community for young people (18-25 year olds) to engage with the arts and culture sector. The collective is for\, of and by young people — to explore our shared identities and interests through art.
DTEND:20260913T073000Z
DTSTART:20260913T053000Z
SUMMARY:Tracing Color
UID:1393@map-india.org
URL:https://map-india.org/map-events/tracing-color/
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR